- decided_at: 2026-09-23T01:54:02+00:00
  event: initial
  value: Docker container backend is the first isolated execution backend: one container per scope, workspace bind-mounted at the canonical host tree, docker exec per command. Prerequisite seam landed first: execution.Backend/BackendScope/ScopeSpec/Launch, Manager.Register, Options.Backend name selection, and RequireIsolation refusing unknown, unisolated, or unavailable backends with no fallback. Scope keeps all authority (policy, command shape, stdin, dir resolution, timeout clamp, process ID) before any backend sees a launch.
  reason: A container contains descendants via the PID namespace, which the local backend explicitly cannot guarantee, and expresses filesystem/network/pids/memory limits as flags instead of three hand-rolled OS implementations. Native primitives would mean AppContainer on Windows, seccomp/landlock on Linux, and deprecated sandbox-exec on macOS - three chances to advertise a boundary weaker than it is. A bind mount keeps Scope.Workspace the host path, so internal/files needs no adapter and there is no copy-back or host-edit conflict detection. Docker Desktop is installed on the dev machine; WSL2 alone was rejected as Windows-only with weak per-run isolation.
